 "observations": [
  "The page number «158» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 47», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. A speck at the left of the titles; not transcribed. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman: N's title, O's «Elzohara», C's «Examinatio Veneris»; L04, «Locus autem draconis eius 1. 29. 27», in smaller bold type, followed by «(CN)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of L03 and L04, «C 66r, O 123v,» and «N 30v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 157 (JP2 0189), with «Sublimatio ascensus1) Veneris examinata2)», «Examinatio ipsius elheza3)», «Examinatio centri», «Statio prima Veneris» and «Latitudo Veneris accedentis4)» over «prima» and «secunda». Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked as on p. 156 (JP2 0188): the Sublimatio is 2s 21° 15′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole. The Latitudo secunda is 5° · sin x to within 0.6″ in every row, and 5° 0′ 0″ at 90°, the greatest latitude of Venus. Its values are those of Saturn's Latitudo secunda (p. 140, JP2 0172) except at 75°, where Saturn has 4° 49′ 46″ for the 4° 49′ 47″ here (4° 49′ 46.7″ computed).",
  "L11-L40, the Examinatio argumenti. The radius that reproduces each value as the equation of an epicycle is between 44.03 and 44.12 in every row, against 43.87 to 44.07 on p. 157 (JP2 0189); against the epicycle of radius 43.95 of p. 156 (JP2 0188) the table is 1.8′ to 5.7′ larger (90°: 36° 19′ for 36° 13.4′). The steps of the column fall from 24′ to 21′ a degree.",
  "L11-L40, the other columns: the Examinatio centri increases by 1′ a degree to 73° and then by 0′ or 1′, to 2° 14′ at 90°, the greatest equation of the sun (Tab. 21, p. 132, JP2 0164); it lies up to 2.0′ below 2° 14′ · sin x. The Statio prima increases from 5s 16° 31′ to 5s 17° 11′ by 1′ to 3′ a degree. The Latitudo prima decreases from 2° 3′ at 61° to 1° 41′ at 90°, by 1′ in most degrees.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 3s 0°, is N's; C and O have 2. Note b: the degrees of the second argument at 90°, 9s 0°, are C's; O has 2. Checked: the Sublimatio 2s 8° 35′ at 61° (note e, C, against O's 25) and 2s 6° 49′ at 70° (note c, O and N, against C's 7) agree with the rule; at 84° (notes d and f) the edition prints 2s 4° 10′, with C's minutes and its own 4 against the 3 of C and O, the value that the Examinatio argumenti of the row, 34° 10′, requires. The Statio prima 5s 16° 31′ at 61° (notes g and h, C's minutes against O's 32, the degrees of O and N against C's 17) continues the 5s 16° 30′ of 60° (p. 157); the Latitudo prima 2° 0′ at 65° (note i, C, against O's 1) fits its column; the Latitudo secunda 4° 34′ 4″ at 66° (note k, C, against O's 33), 4° 34′ 3.8″ computed.",
  "Notes on the readings, continued. The seconds of the Latitudo secunda at 65°, 73°, 87° and 88° (notes l, m, n, o) are the edition's, against the 13, 13, 45 and 39 of C and O: 4° 31′ 53″, 4° 46′ 53″, 4° 59′ 35″ and 4° 59′ 49″, the values of Saturn at the same arguments (p. 140, JP2 0172), against 4° 31′ 53.5″, 4° 46′ 53.5″, 4° 59′ 35.3″ and 4° 59′ 49.0″ computed. The notes do not say «correxi».",
  "Italics: «om.». N01-N02: «Ve-neris», divided at the end of the line with a hyphen."
 ],
